-- Applied on 2026-11-04 through the signed-in admin panel, with the same resulting values as the statements below.
-- Site update of 2026-11-04: experience, skills, MSR-SLAM publication, payload project figures from the final paper,
-- project dates and titles. The previous rows are not recorded here; the paper-withheld DJI project text is untouched.
begin;

update public.projects set title = $q$Offline Multi-System AI Payload for Search & Rescue Robots$q$, start_date = null, end_date = '09/2026',
  tools = ARRAY[$q$NVIDIA Jetson TX2$q$,$q$STM32F411 sensor hub$q$,$q$MiCS-5524 gas sensor and flame sensor$q$,$q$FLIR Lepton 3 thermal camera$q$,$q$YOLOv11n with TensorRT$q$,$q$24 GHz FMCW radar$q$,$q$Gemma 3 1B with llama.cpp$q$,$q$Faster-Whisper speech recognition$q$,$q$Piper text-to-speech$q$,$q$Ebyte E22-900T22D LoRa radio$q$,$q$STM32F103 receiver node$q$,$q$ROS 2, Nav2 and Cartographer$q$,$q$TurtleBot3 Waffle Pi$q$,$q$TurtleBot2 Kobuki$q$]::text[],
  outcomes = ARRAY[$q$Works fully offline: detection, voice dialogue and alerts need no internet$q$,$q$Victim detector reaches 96.1% mAP@50, 92.4% precision and 89.7% recall at 26.3 FPS on the Jetson TX2$q$,$q$Thermal gating cut false positives by 84%$q$,$q$Voice response in about 7.2 s after a fixed ~5 s audio capture$q$,$q$LoRa alerts: 99.17% packet delivery (1,785/1,800 packets, 18 runs)$q$,$q$SIGKILL state machine: 0% out-of-memory crashes, 71% faster mode switching$q$,$q$The same payload moves between two different robot bases without modification$q$]::text[],
  rich_content = $q$<h2>The problem</h2><p>In the 2023 Kahramanmaraş earthquakes, cellular and internet networks failed within minutes. Most AI-assisted rescue tools depend on the cloud, so they stop working in exactly that situation. They also tend to rely on a single camera, and they rarely talk to the trapped person.</p><h2>The idea</h2><p>A self-contained payload that bolts onto a ROS 2 robot as an external &ldquo;AI brain&rdquo;. It finds people, talks to them, watches for hazards and reports back, while running entirely on the robot.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/payload-prototype.jpg" alt="The AI payload prototype with LiDAR, speaker, sensor board and camera box" /><figcaption>The payload prototype: LiDAR, speaker, sensor board and a camera box carrying the thermal and visual cameras.</figcaption></figure><h2>What it does</h2><ul><li><strong>Finds victims</strong> by combining a thermal camera, a visual detector and a radar sensor.</li><li><strong>Talks to them</strong> through an offline voice assistant that gives calm, supportive guidance until help arrives.</li><li><strong>Watches for danger</strong> with gas, flame and temperature sensors, and keeps the robot away from hazards.</li><li><strong>Reports back</strong> over Wi-Fi for maps and video, and over a long-range LoRa link when Wi-Fi drops.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/architecture.png" alt="System architecture from sensors to decisions to actions" /><figcaption>System architecture: sensors feed the detector and the decision state machine, which drives navigation, the voice assistant and LoRa alerts.</figcaption></figure><h2>Seeing victims</h2><p>A low-power thermal camera watches continuously and only wakes the heavier visual detector when it sees body heat. This saves energy and cuts false alarms. The detector was trained for 50 epochs on 4,500+ annotated images from the RAI Lab dataset, showing people lying on the floor, partly hidden or in dim light, because public datasets mostly show people standing.</p><p>YOLOv11n with TensorRT FP16 reaches 96.1% mAP@50, 92.4% precision and 89.7% recall at 26.3 FPS, 7.5× faster than the PyTorch baseline. VRAM use drops from 3.1 GB to 1.8 GB, and thermal gating cut false positives by 84%.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/training-dataset.jpg" alt="Training images of people lying in simulated disaster conditions" /><figcaption>Part of the RAI Lab training data: people in simulated disaster postures and lighting.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/detection-confirmed.jpg" alt="A person under a desk confirmed by the visual detector next to the thermal view" /><figcaption>A person partly hidden under a desk: the thermal view (right) triggers the visual detector (left), which confirms the person.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/no-heat-mannequin.jpg" alt="A mannequin standing in front of the robot, with no heat signature" /><figcaption>A mannequin in front of the robot: with no body heat, nothing was triggered, so no false alarm was raised.</figcaption></figure><h2>Sensing through barriers</h2><p>A 24 GHz FMCW bio-radar can pick up the tiny chest movement of breathing, even when the person is hidden. Preliminary tests reached a presence-detection rate of about 94%, at up to 4 m and through light barriers, under controlled lab conditions.</p><p>Because these results are preliminary, the radar is used only as a confirmation sensor after a visual or audio trigger, never as a stand-alone search tool, and the figures describe the lab setup, not rubble or reinforced concrete.</p><h2>Talking to victims</h2><p>Once a victim is confirmed, the robot switches to voice mode. Speech is converted to text, a small language model prepares a short supportive reply, and a text-to-speech voice speaks it. All three steps run locally on the robot: Faster-Whisper (a quantized Whisper model) turns speech into text in 2.35–2.57 seconds, Gemma 3 1B with llama.cpp writes the reply in 3.09–3.44 seconds, and Piper speaks it in about 1.5 seconds. After a fixed audio capture of about 5 seconds, the robot therefore responds in about 7.2 seconds. The two heavy models cannot fit in memory together, so a SIGKILL-based state machine switches between them: 0% out-of-memory crashes, 71% faster mode switching, and the next model loaded in about 0.3 seconds.</p><h2>Staying safe</h2><p>A separate STM32F411 microcontroller reads the MiCS-5524 gas sensor, a flame sensor and temperature sensors on a fixed schedule, so sensing is never blocked by the AI workload. Temperatures above 60 °C mark no-go zones in the Nav2 costmap, and the robot plans a route around the area.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/sensor-board.jpg" alt="The sensor board with STM32 microcontroller, gas, flame and temperature sensors and LoRa radio" /><figcaption>The sensor board built around an STM32 microcontroller, with gas, flame, temperature and motion sensors and the LoRa radio.</figcaption></figure><h2>Saving power</h2><p>Wake-on-Suspect keeps the GPU inactive until a thermal trigger; only the ~150 mW thermal camera runs during patrol.</p><h2>Staying connected</h2><p>Wi-Fi carries maps and video when coverage exists. An Ebyte E22-900T22D LoRa radio (22 dBm, 2,400 bps, 26-byte payload) carries compact status messages (robot position, victim status and hazards) when Wi-Fi is lost, to an STM32F103 receiver node at the command center. Alerts and hazards show up on a command-centre screen.</p><ul><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> 99.17% packet delivery (1,785/1,800 packets, 18 runs): indoor 99.75% across up to 6 floors, outdoor 98.00%.</li><li><strong>Integrity:</strong> XOR checksum + CRC-16-CCITT: 0 failed, malformed or duplicate frames.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/operations-map.jpg" alt="The command-centre screen showing the robot trail, a flame alert and sensor readings" /><figcaption>The command-centre dashboard in a simulation run: robot trail, a flame alert zone and sensor readings.</figcaption></figure><h2>Two different robots</h2><p>To show that the payload is not tied to one robot, it was mounted on two different bases: a TurtleBot3 Waffle Pi and a TurtleBot2 Kobuki, both working through the same ROS 2 interface.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/platform-turtlebot3.jpg" alt="The payload mounted on a TurtleBot3 Waffle Pi" /><figcaption>On a TurtleBot3 Waffle Pi, the main test platform.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/platform-kobuki.jpg" alt="The payload mounted on a TurtleBot2 Kobuki" /><figcaption>On a TurtleBot2 Kobuki, used to check portability.</figcaption></figure><h2>From simulation to the real robot</h2><p>Navigation and mapping were first tried in a Gazebo simulation with collapsed obstacles, narrow corridors and simulated victims, then moved to the physical robots. The robot builds a map as it moves, heads towards a confirmed victim, and avoids zones flagged as dangerous.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/simulation-rviz.jpg" alt="Gazebo simulation next to the RViz occupancy map" /><figcaption>Gazebo simulation (left) and the live occupancy map in RViz (right).</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/offline-ai-payload/operational-flow.png" alt="Operational flowchart from thermal scan to voice mode and sleep" /><figcaption>The operating loop: thermal scan, wake the detector, confirm a victim, send a LoRa alert, then switch to voice mode and back to sleep.</figcaption></figure><h2>Honest limits</h2><p>These are lab results on indoor research robots. The detector was trained on simulated disaster scenes, the radar was only tested in the lab and works as a confirmation sensor, and the voice assistant responds in about 7.2 seconds after a fixed audio capture of about 5 seconds. Testing in real rubble and on rugged robot bodies is the next step.</p><h2>Team</h2><p>The project was built solely by Mohammed Bajhaw at the RAI Laboratory, Mechatronics Engineering Department, Fırat University, under the supervision of Prof. Dr. Ayşegül Uçar. Munef El Muhammed and Anas Mahyoub Naji Saeed Alqadhi are co-authors of the research paper, not members of the project team.</p>$q$, updated_at = now()
where slug = 'offline-multi-sensor-ai-payload-for-usar';

update public.projects set start_date = null, end_date = '11/2025', tools = ARRAY[$q$ROS 2 Humble$q$,$q$Ignition Gazebo Fortress$q$,$q$ros_gz bridge$q$,$q$3D LiDAR$q$,$q$OctoMap$q$,$q$D*+/DSP planner$q$,$q$TF2$q$,$q$RViz2$q$,$q$C++$q$,$q$Python$q$,$q$Ubuntu 22.04$q$]::text[],
  rich_content = $q$<h2>What the project is about</h2><p>A drone flying through a cave cannot use GPS, cannot count on light, and has walls only a metre or two away on every side. This project gives it a way to cope: it builds a 3D map while it flies, plans a safe path through that map, chooses where to explore next and steers along the path on its own. Everything was developed and tested in simulation.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/tunnel-octomap.jpg" alt="Coloured 3D voxel map of a tunnel built live from the drone's LiDAR" /><figcaption>The tunnel as the drone sees it: a 3D map built live from LiDAR, coloured by height.</figcaption></figure><h2>How it works</h2><ul><li><strong>Mapping:</strong> a 3D LiDAR on the drone feeds OctoMap, which turns the laser points into a grid of small cubes marking free and occupied space.</li><li><strong>Planning:</strong> a D*+/DSP planner searches that 3D grid for a route with no collisions, and repairs the route when new obstacles appear instead of starting again.</li><li><strong>Exploring:</strong> a frontier explorer finds the edge between mapped and unknown space, picks a safe spot there and sends it to the planner as the next goal.</li><li><strong>Flying:</strong> a tunnel controller follows the planned path by looking ahead along it, holds a safe altitude and keeps away from the walls.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://bajhaw.com/figures/cave-drone-architecture.png" alt="System architecture from simulator and bridge through mapping, planning and flight control" /><figcaption>How the pieces connect: the simulator and bridge feed mapping, the planner and explorer decide where to go, and the controller turns the path into flight commands.</figcaption></figure><h2>Staying out of trouble</h2><p>Caves are full of dead ends, so the system is built to notice and recover rather than assume the plan always works.</p><ul><li>The planner keeps a safety margin around every obstacle and smooths the path so the drone never has to make a sharp turn.</li><li>The explorer rejects targets that sit too close to a wall.</li><li>If the drone is told to move but barely moves for a couple of seconds, it stops, reverses and turns to give the LiDAR a new view, then plans again.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-mission.jpg" alt="RViz showing the 3D map during a mission next to the planner and controller terminals" /><figcaption>A mission in progress: the 3D map in RViz, with the planner and flight-controller terminals alongside.</figcaption></figure><h2>Maps and test worlds</h2><p>The map is stored as small cubes, so the planner works with solid obstacles instead of millions of raw laser points. The system was exercised in several simulated worlds: a rough cave, a mine, a collapsed building and a warehouse.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/octomap-closeup.jpg" alt="Occupancy voxels around the drone in RViz" /><figcaption>Occupied space around the drone, close up.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/gazebo-warehouse.jpg" alt="The simulated quadrotor in a warehouse test world" /><figcaption>The simulated quadrotor in one of the test worlds.</figcaption></figure><h2>Main settings</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Map cell size</td><td>10 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Planner update</td><td>about every 0.8 s</td></tr><tr><td>Hover height</td><td>0.8 m</td></tr><tr><td>Top speed</td><td>0.8 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Look-ahead distance</td><td>1.0 m</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A single command starts the whole mission: the simulator, the transform bridge, mapping and planning, the flight controller and the explorer, each in its own terminal.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-displays.jpg" alt="RViz with the occupancy grid, path and TF displays enabled" /><figcaption>The RViz setup used while developing: occupancy grid, planned path and coordinate frames.</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it stands</h2><p>This is a simulation project. The repository documents the system and its tuning values, but there is no formal benchmark of success rate or area covered, so none is claimed here. The planning and control parts are separate from the simulator, so moving them to a real vehicle is a matter of replacing the simulated sensors and flight interface.</p><p>Code, launch instructions and configuration are in the <a href="https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/drone-navigation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">project repository</a>.</p>$q$, updated_at = now()
where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone';
update public.project_media set storage_path = $q$https://bajhaw.com/figures/cave-drone-architecture.png$q$ where project_id = 6 and storage_path like '%architecture.png';

update public.projects set title = $q$5-Axis Robotic Arm: SolidWorks Design to Simscape Control$q$, start_date = null, end_date = '01/2026',
  description = $q$Designing a robotic arm is more than drawing parts: you also need to know it can move smoothly, carry its own weight and reach the point you ask for. This project follows the whole path, from a multi-link arm designed in SolidWorks to a physics-based simulation in MATLAB Simscape Multibody.

Two models are included. A simple two-joint arm is used to learn PID tuning, and a 5-axis robotic arm with a two-finger gripper is driven by inverse kinematics.$q$, rich_content = $q$<h2>What the project is about</h2><p>A robotic arm has to do more than look right: its parts must move smoothly, carry their own weight and reach the point you ask for. This project follows that whole path. The arm is designed in SolidWorks, brought into MATLAB as a physics model, and then given the maths to move to a target position.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/assembly-wide.png" alt="The five-part robotic arm assembled from the SolidWorks models" /><figcaption>The arm assembled from the exported SolidWorks parts (rendered from the project's CAD files).</figcaption></figure><h2>From CAD to a physical model</h2><p>The arm was modelled as separate parts in SolidWorks and assembled. The assembly carries each part's mass, centre of mass and inertia, so when it is imported into Simscape Multibody the simulated arm feels gravity and joint loads like a real one. When the arm is stretched out, for example, the base joint really does need more torque to hold the pose.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/parts-sheet.png" alt="The individual SolidWorks parts of the arm" /><figcaption>The CAD parts: base, links, joint housing and the gripper finger, each shown on its own.</figcaption></figure><h2>Two models</h2><ul><li><strong>2-DOF arm:</strong> the PID-control study model, a simple two-joint arm: set a target point and watch how the gains change overshoot and settling.</li><li><strong>Advanced arm:</strong> a 5-axis robotic arm with a two-finger gripper, moved by inverse kinematics.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/tutorial-2dof.png" alt="The simpler 2-DOF tutorial arm" /><figcaption>The 2-DOF tutorial arm.</figcaption></figure><h2>Telling the arm where to go</h2><p>Instead of guessing each joint angle, you give the arm a point in space (X, Y, Z in millimetres) and the inverse kinematics script works out the angles. The base turns toward the target, and the shoulder and elbow are solved from the arm's link lengths with simple trigonometry. The wrist is then tilted to keep the gripper level. If the target is out of reach, the script warns and clamps it to the arm's maximum reach.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/assembly-side.png" alt="Side view of the assembled robotic arm" /><figcaption>Side view of the arm.</figcaption></figure><h2>Making it simulate reliably</h2><ul><li>Setup scripts load the model, fix the base to the table, lock the gripper joints and add the angle inputs, so the model is ready with one command.</li><li>CAD imports often fail with a &ldquo;degenerate mass&rdquo; error when massless parts are joined directly. A tiny extra mass body between the joints (IntermediateMass) fixes it without changing the arm.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/workflow.png" alt="Workflow from SolidWorks CAD through Simscape, inverse kinematics and PID control" /><figcaption>The workflow: CAD to physical model, target point to joint angles, and control on top.</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it stands</h2><p>The arm moves in step jumps to each target rather than along a smooth trajectory, and the joint-angle offsets between the maths and the CAD model are marked for calibration in the scripts. Natural next steps are smooth trajectory planning, camera-based object detection and moving the control to ROS 2 for hardware tests.</p><p>CAD files, MATLAB scripts and setup instructions are in the <a href="https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/Robotic-Arm-SolidWorks-Matlab" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">project repository</a>.</p>$q$, updated_at = now()
where slug = 'robotic-arm-cad-simulation-control';

update public.project_media set alt = $q$The 5-axis robotic arm assembled from the SolidWorks models$q$ where id = 28;

update public.projects set start_date = null, end_date = '06/2025', rich_content = $q$
<h2>System architecture</h2>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/automated-coffee-system_b0886379.png" alt="Architecture diagram of the Raspberry Pi automated coffee preparation system, including interface, relays, servos, boiler, pump, and dispensers" />
  <figcaption>Embedded control architecture linking the Raspberry Pi, operator interface, relay-switched loads, PWM actuators, and remote maintenance path.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>The Automated Coffee Preparation System was designed as a coordinated embedded-control prototype rather than a collection of disconnected devices.</strong> A <strong>Raspberry Pi</strong> served as the control core, connecting user selection, dispensing actuators, pumping, and heating-related stages within one modular software and hardware arrangement.</p>

<h2>Control concept</h2>
<p>The system was developed at <strong>Fırat University</strong> in June 2025. Its control software follows a <strong>modular object-oriented Python</strong> approach so that preparation stages and connected devices can be organised as distinct responsibilities. This structure supports changes to device behaviour without collapsing the sequence logic into one monolithic program.</p>

<h2>Power and actuation integration</h2>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/coffee-electronics-original_57c3893d.jpg" alt="Electronics prototype with relay module, wiring, control board, and compact actuators for the automated coffee preparation system" />
  <figcaption>Project electronics prototype showing relay, wiring, controller-board, and actuator integration context.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The documented electrical design separates the connected loads by function. Relay stages control the <strong>220 V AC boiler</strong> and <strong>12 V DC pump</strong>, while <strong>PWM servo actuators</strong> control the coffee and milk dispensing mechanisms. This division keeps the automation logic connected to the distinct electrical and mechanical behaviours required for the preparation sequence.</p>

<h2>Local interaction and maintainability</h2>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/coffee-front-panel-original_f36eb82c.jpg" alt="Completed front panel and local-selection interface for the automated coffee preparation system" />
  <figcaption>Completed front panel and local-selection interface, shown with the local-operation and maintenance discussion.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A <strong>16×2 LCD</strong> and <strong>keypad</strong> provide a compact local interface for selecting preparation options. For development and maintenance, the software can be reached through <strong>SSH over Ethernet</strong>, enabling headless debugging and updates without requiring a dedicated display or keyboard at the controller.</p>

<h2>Documented outcomes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Connected relay-switched boiler and pump stages with PWM dispensing actuators under one <strong>Raspberry Pi</strong> workflow.</li>
  <li>Organised preparation behaviour in <strong>modular Python</strong> rather than a single hard-to-maintain control script.</li>
  <li>Combined a practical keypad/LCD interface with Ethernet-based remote maintenance.</li>
</ul>
$q$, updated_at = now() where slug = 'smart-coffee-brewing-system';

update public.projects set start_date = null, end_date = '03/2025', rich_content = $q$
<h2>System architecture</h2>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/cnc-multi-function-system_3c6764e0.png" alt="Architecture diagram of the CNC multi-function conversion workflow from CAD and CAM to an interchangeable-tool machine" />
  <figcaption>Documented workflow from mechanical redesign and CAM preparation to the converted machine frame and interchangeable tool heads.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>The engineering aim was to extend an existing 3D-printer platform into a configurable fabrication system.</strong> Instead of assigning one fixed head to the machine, the conversion was structured around an interchangeable-tool concept that maps one motion platform to three documented tasks: <strong>wood carving</strong>, <strong>plastic engraving</strong>, and <strong>2D drawing</strong>.</p>

<h2>Design objective and project context</h2>
<p>The project was completed at <strong>Fırat University</strong> in March 2025 with collaborator <strong>Anas Alqadhi</strong>, under the supervision of <strong>Assoc. Prof. Dr. Haşim Pıhtılı</strong>. Its scope combined mechanical adaptation, digital toolpath preparation, and workshop-oriented calibration so that a compact machine could be configured around more than one fabrication activity.</p>

<h2>Mechanical redesign and interchangeable tooling</h2>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/linkedin-project-3_b8ccee20.jpg" alt="CAD rendering of the enclosed mechanical assembly used as design-stage documentation for the CNC conversion" />
  <figcaption>CAD-stage mechanical assembly image recorded with the project; used here to contextualise the redesign phase.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The conversion began with a <strong>SolidWorks</strong>-based mechanical redesign of the original printer arrangement. The design had to preserve the platform's motion capability while making the end effector adaptable to the required tooling. The resulting operating concept accommodates a drill-style engraving head, a heated pen, and a writing pen, allowing the same gantry system to be prepared for different material and mark-making tasks.</p>

<h2>Digital fabrication workflow</h2>
<p>For two-dimensional work, vector assets were prepared in <strong>Inkscape</strong> and transferred through <strong>JSCut</strong> to generate G-code. <strong>Ultimaker Cura</strong> was used to optimise movement paths, linking the mechanical platform to a repeatable preparation sequence rather than relying on ad hoc manual motion commands. This chain makes the relationship between design input, toolpath generation, and physical execution explicit.</p>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/cnc-engraving-operation-original_df74f5e2.jpg" alt="Close project image of a machining or tool-operation setup supporting the CNC fabrication workflow" />
  <figcaption>Project documentation of a tool-operation setup, placed with the CAM-to-fabrication discussion.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Fabrication and calibration</h2>
<p>The conversion was calibrated across the materials and tasks documented for the project. This step connected motion settings, tool selection, and the response of wood and plastic workpieces, while the drawing head provided a lower-force mode for two-dimensional output. The emphasis was on practical configuration of a multi-use platform rather than unsupported performance comparisons.</p>
<figure>
  <img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/legacy/cnc-3d-model-print-original_09f25375.jpg" alt="Physical CNC conversion prototype showing tool or motor assemblies" />
  <figcaption>Physical prototype documentation showing the tool or motor assembly used in the interchangeable-head concept.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Documented outcomes and next direction</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Converted a 3D-printer platform into a <strong>multi-function CNC concept</strong> with interchangeable operation modes.</li>
  <li>Established a clear <strong>SVG → CAM → G-code → machine</strong> preparation path for the documented tasks.</li>
  <li>Recorded calibration work for wood carving, plastic engraving, and 2D drawing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Future directions proposed in the original project description include <strong>AI-assisted engraving</strong> and <strong>automated tool changing</strong>. These are presented as development goals, not as capabilities claimed for the completed prototype.</p>
$q$, updated_at = now() where slug = 'high-precision-cnc-router';

update public.publications set status = 'Published · Journal Article', sort_order = 1,
  summary = $q$A multi-stage refinement pipeline that improves the reliability of LiDAR-inertial SLAM in GPS-denied environments.$q$,
  details = $q$<h2>Abstract</h2><p>LiDAR–inertial odometry can remain locally accurate while accumulating unbounded global drift, and in structurally repetitive GPS-denied environments, the loop closures intended to remove that drift may instead corrupt the map. This paper presents MSR-SLAM, a multi-stage map-refinement framework built on an existing LiDAR–inertial odometry front-end. Loop candidates retrieved by a learned place-recognition descriptor are verified geometrically. In the offline batch configuration, geometrically verified loop constraints are incorporated into pose graph optimization after the complete sequence is available. In the online configuration, geometrically verified candidates pass through an additional admission stage that rejects constraints whose candidate-induced translational or rotational correction exceeds preset limits before they can affect the active pose graph. The global map is subsequently reconstructed from the optimized poses. The main real-robot evaluation comprised seven offline/online processing cases derived from five distinct recorded traversals in indoor, outdoor, and subterranean environments, using a Unitree Go2 quadruped carrying a 16-beam LiDAR. Additional live traversals were used separately for the online consistency analysis. In one online sequence, 14 of the 15 geometrically verified candidates were rejected at the admission stage; in a loop-free tunnel, all 310 retrieved candidates were rejected during geometric verification, and the trajectory was left unmodified. The offline failure case E4 combined excellent registration quality and large residual reduction with severe global distortion and no loop edge spanning 500 or more node indices, highlighting the need to assess loop-edge coverage alongside local fit. Three independent traversals of one environment produced geometric acceptance rates between 10.8% and 11.2%, with exactly one loop constraint admitted in each traversal. On KITTI Sequence 00, MSR-SLAM reduced the RKO-LIO ATE RMSE from 9.5525 m to 4.9322 m (48.4%) while leaving the short-term RPE essentially unchanged.</p><h2>Featured application</h2><p>The framework supports post-disaster and subterranean survey missions in which a legged robot must build a globally consistent three-dimensional map without satellite positioning, and in which an incorrect map correction is more damaging than an uncorrected one.</p><h2>In short</h2><p>A robot that maps with LiDAR slowly drifts, and loop closures are meant to fix that drift. In corridors, basements and tunnels, where places look alike, a wrong loop closure can ruin the map instead. MSR-SLAM treats the hard problem as deciding which loop closures deserve to change the map: candidates are retrieved by a learned descriptor, verified geometrically, and, when running online, a further check blocks any constraint that would force an implausibly large correction.</p><h2>Main contributions</h2><ul><li><strong>Multi-stage framework:</strong> place recognition, geometric verification, pose graph optimization and map reconstruction, run offline in batch or online in ROS 2 with motion-gated keyframes.</li><li><strong>Online admission control:</strong> each verified loop candidate is checked for the correction it would cause, and rejected above 0.7 m or 15 degrees before it can touch the active graph.</li><li><strong>A warning about local metrics:</strong> registration quality and residual reduction alone do not show that a refinement is correct.</li><li><strong>Negative-control evidence:</strong> a loop-free tunnel where nothing was admitted, and three independent traversals of one environment.</li></ul><h2>How it works</h2><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/framework-overview.png" alt="Overview of the MSR-SLAM framework" /><figcaption>Framework overview: the RKO-LIO front-end feeds a refinement back-end shared by the offline batch and online ROS 2 configurations. Grey blocks are adopted from prior work.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/admission-cascade.png" alt="Online three-stage filtering cascade and candidate attrition" /><figcaption>The online filtering cascade (left) and how candidates were whittled down in three online experiments (right).</figcaption></figure><h2>Real-robot experiments</h2><p>The system was tested on a Unitree Go2 quadruped carrying a 16-beam Hesai PandarXT-16 LiDAR and the robot's built-in IMU. Five recorded traversals, processed as seven offline and online cases, covered corridors, a basement, an outdoor building side and a subterranean tunnel.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/unitree-go2-platform.png" alt="Unitree Go2 quadruped with a Hesai PandarXT-16 LiDAR" /><figcaption>The Unitree Go2 quadruped with the 16-beam LiDAR.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/test-environments.png" alt="Representative views of the four test environments" /><figcaption>Test environments: (a) indoor corridor, (b) basement, (c) outdoor building side, (d) subterranean tunnel.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key findings</h2><ul><li><strong>An offline failure, reported openly:</strong> in sequence E4 the best registration scores and the largest residual reduction came with the worst global distortion, because no loop edge spanned 500 or more node indices. Local fit alone can hide a failing map.</li><li><strong>Admission control at work:</strong> online, 14 of 15 geometrically verified candidates in one sequence were stopped before reaching the graph. Without admission control, 14 edges were admitted instead of one and the maximum correction rose from 0.283 m to 1.235 m.</li><li><strong>Negative control:</strong> in a loop-free tunnel all 310 retrieved candidates were rejected by geometric verification and the trajectory was left unchanged.</li><li><strong>Repeatability:</strong> three independent traversals gave geometric acceptance rates of 10.8% to 11.2%, with exactly one loop constraint admitted each time.</li><li><strong>Physical check:</strong> four distances measured with a laser meter in one environment improved from 0.331 m to 0.031 m mean absolute error, a limited check dominated by one measurement.</li></ul><h2>Benchmark on KITTI</h2><p>On KITTI Sequence 00, MSR-SLAM reduced the RKO-LIO absolute trajectory error from 9.5525 m to 4.9322 m (48.4%) and the SE(3)-aligned error from 3.6584 m to 1.9395 m, while the short-term relative error stayed essentially unchanged. The benefit grows with segment length, which fits a method that suppresses accumulated drift rather than changing local odometry. The baseline methods were run with loop closure disabled, so this measures the gain from adding the back-end to RKO-LIO.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/publications/msr-slam/kitti-trajectories.png" alt="Estimated trajectories on KITTI Sequence 00 compared with the reference" /><figcaption>KITTI Sequence 00: estimated trajectories against the reference. The MSR-SLAM trajectory stays closest to it over long distances.</figcaption></figure><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The robot experiments have no external ground truth, so absolute error is not reported. They use one platform and one LiDAR model, the place-recognition network was trained on 64-beam data, only three traversals were repeated, and the correctness of individual rejections was not independently verified.</p><h2>Full paper</h2><p>Published in Applied Sciences (MDPI), 2026.</p><p>Funded by TÜBİTAK (grant 123E406) and the Fırat University Scientific Research Projects Unit (FÜBAP, grants MF.24.80, MF.25.154 and MF.25.155). The study was conducted as part of the master's thesis of Munef El Muhammed.</p>$q$
